    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Was</strong> and <strong>were</strong> are the past of <em>am / is / are</em>. Use them for the past: <em>Yesterday I was tired. We were in Cardiff last week.</em> Time words help: <em>yesterday, last week, in 2019, two years ago</em>.",
        "tag": "Past of be"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>was</strong> for <em>I, he, she, it</em>: <em>I was at home. She was ill. It was cold.</em> <strong>were</strong> for <em>you, we, they</em>: <em>You were late. We were happy. They were in York.</em> There are only two forms.",
        "tag": "was or were"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Negative:</strong> <em>was not = wasn't, were not = weren't</em>: <em>He wasn't at work. We weren't at the party.</em> <strong>Question:</strong> put <em>was / were</em> first: <em>Was she ill? Were you at home?</em> <strong>Short answers:</strong> <em>Yes, I was. No, we weren't.</em>",
        "tag": "No and questions"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>ago</strong> comes after the time: <em>two years ago, a week ago</em>. The time word can start the sentence or end it: <em>Two years ago I was in Belfast. I was in Belfast two years ago.</em> Never say ✗ <em>before two years</em>.",
        "tag": "Time words"
      },
      {
        "html": "Bonus: <em>have</em> becomes <strong>had</strong> for every person: <em>I had a cold. She had a car. We had no time.</em> After <em>didn't</em> or <em>did</em> use <em>have</em>, not <em>had</em>: <em>I didn't have time.</em>",
        "tag": "Bonus: had"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>I were at home.</em> → ✓ <em>I was at home.</em> · ✗ <em>Was you at the party?</em> → ✓ <em>Were you at the party?</em> · ✗ <em>I didn't was there.</em> → ✓ <em>I wasn't there.</em> Be is the only verb that has two past forms. For other verbs, see <a href=\"/perfekt\">the past simple</a>. For the present, see <a href=\"/personalpronomen\">subject pronouns and be</a>.",
